>tell everyone to get down
"Everybody," you say, and your voice comes out wrong, "get down. Now. Under the
table. Please." To your surprise, Tricia, Ford and Random all do. The man in the
raincoat watches you, and then the table, and then, with a small nod, you.

Random picks up the pistol to hand it to you, and her thumb finds the one
control she hasn't read about. There is a click, a whine, and a line of white
light that makes the air smell of toast.

The beam wanders. It touches the beams, a bottle, the corner table. A man in a
damp raincoat is no longer sitting at it. Because you warned them, everybody is
already under the table, and nobody else is hurt; the pub erupts in shouting and
a barman diving for the cellar, but it is the sort of eruption one can watch
from underneath.

[The score has just gone up by ten points.]

>sit
You steer the others to the corner table. Tricia takes the chair that faces the
door, from habit. Ford takes the one that faces the exit, from a different
habit. Random pulls out the one nearest the wall and sits with her bag in her
lap, looking like someone who has agreed to this under protest. For the first
time since you landed, nobody is running.

[The score has just gone up by five points.]

>take matchbook
Taken.

>examine matchbook
The cover bears the pub's name and an address. Inside, in the cramped hand of a
man who kept careful count of everything, are a date and a time. You know the
date. You know the time. Years ago, in some other context, a furious voice told
you where it expected to meet its last end, and at what hour: here, this table,
this minute. A creature who had come back and back and back, as one small animal
after another, each of them killed by something you happened to be doing. The
score is settled. Whatever it was, it is finished. For the first time since a
bulldozer in a country lane, a very old knot inside you comes loose.

Ford has been reading over your shoulder. He begins to laugh, the laugh of a man
who has just seen the last piece of a very long joke arrive.

[The score has just gone up by fifteen points.]

>put fish in ear
The fish slides into your ear with the faint reluctance of something that has
already guessed what you are about to ask of it, and for a moment the whole pub
goes quiet in the way a church does. Then you feel it: a vast number of small,
bored, golden creatures, in a vast number of ears, all quietly deciding that
this is not the afternoon on which the Galaxy gets to wind itself up. Somewhere
an improbability that has been building for decades curdles, politely, into
something kinder, and the Whole Sort of General Mish Mash, for once, sorts
itself out.

There is a pop.

You are sitting on a velvet chair at a table in an enormous domed restaurant.
Beyond a vast window the universe is ending, magnificently, the way it does
every night; nobody is looking.

The Dining Hall
An enormous domed hall of white linen and polished brass, under a ceiling that
has been painted with the night sky and then, at some point, switched on. Tables
run off into a golden distance. On a small stage at the far end, a band is
playing something faintly apologetic. Your own table, round and large and
beautifully laid, stands in the middle. The foyer is south, the kitchen east
through a swing door, and glass doors to the north open onto a terrace.

Tricia McMillan is here, watching everyone with a journalist's patient eye. 

Random lounges against the wall with her arms folded, looking at everything the
way a cat looks at a bath. 

Ford is here, restless, taking in the place as though pricing it. 

Marvin stands beside your chair with a tray, in the way of someone who has been
waiting a very long time for no one to ask. 

Random, who has not said a word since the pop, reaches into her coat and hands
you something folded very small. "I took it from your hut," she says, not
looking at you. "On the first day. I wanted to know what you looked at." It is
the pencil sketch. She has smoothed it flat.

[The score has just gone up by ten points.]

>order salad
"An excellent choice," says the waiter, in a tone that suggests it is the first
he has heard in some centuries. He writes it down with enormous ceremony. The
Dish of the Day nods approvingly and wanders off to wait for someone less
principled. "You are," says Ford, with feeling, "a better man than I am."

[The score has just gone up by eight points.]

>ask marvin about his diodes
"The diodes," says Marvin, as though you had finally said the magic word. "Down
my left side. Since you ask, which nobody has. They have been aching since
before the planet you're thinking of had a name." A pause, in which he seems to
enjoy it. "Thank you. I feel no better. But it is something to have said." He
lifts his tray a quarter-inch, which is how he indicates that he will come
along, naturally, and that no one will notice.

[The score has just gone up by six points.]

